We first describe the essential components used in this line of research before explaining relevant research works.
\subsection{Diffusion Models}
Diffusion models are a class of generative models that learn to reverse the Markov chain diffusion process. Let $x_0$ represent true data observations and $x_t$ represent intermediate noised data, when $t = T$, corresponding observations $x_T$ are noised to become Gaussian noise. More precisely, diffusion process<|CIT-MASK|> is defined as
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
q\left(x_t \mid x_{t-1}\right) & := \mathcal{N}\left(x_t ; \sqrt{\alpha_t} x_{t-1},\left(1-\alpha_t\right) \mathbf{I}\right) \\
q(x_T|x_0)&\approx\mathcal{N}(x_T;\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}), \\
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\alpha_t$ is a fixed<|CIT-MASK|> or learnable schedule<|CIT-MASK|>. According to Bayes' rule, we can obtain the reverse diffusion, which can be interpreted as an interpolation between $x_t$ and $x_0$. Then, we can learn to predict this distribution by matching it with a parameterized network and minimizing the KL divergence of the two distributions. The divergence of two Gaussian distributions can be formulated as the mean square error loss. In practice, we reparameterize $x_t$ so that we predict the epsilon $\epsilon_t$<|CIT-MASK|> that was used to generate $x_t$ as follows:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_\text{diffusion}=\mathbb{E}_{x_t, t, \epsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1)}\left[\left\|\epsilon-\epsilon_\theta\left(x_t, t\right)\right\|_2^2\right]
\end{equation}

