By diffusing in the latent space of powerful VAEs<|CIT-MASK|> and conditioning these models with text embeddings<|CIT-MASK|>, they take the form of Latent Diffusion Models (LDM)<|CIT-MASK|> or commonly known as “text-to-image diffusion models”. With the addition of these two components, the loss can be formulated as follows:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_\text{LDM}=\mathbb{E}_{z_t \in \mathcal{E}(x), t, c, \epsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1)}\left[\left\|\epsilon-\epsilon_\theta\left(z_t, c, t\right)\right\|_2^2\right],
\end{equation}
where $z_t$ is the noised latent embedding of image $x$ through a VAE, and $c$ is the text embedding encoded by text encoders such as CLIP<|CIT-MASK|>. 

